<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="extension-methods" title="Extension methods">

    <secondary-label ref="experimental"/>

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        An extension is a method or a constant on a type you do not own. One holder class
        contributes it. That type is never modified; your call sites are.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["Holder class marked @Extension"] --> B["Processor records one manifest entry per contribution"]
    B --> C["Stubs goal patches a copy of each receiver type"]
    C --> D["javac compiles your call against that copy"]
    D --> E["Weave goal repoints every instruction at its holder"]
    E --> F["Woven caller invokes the static implementation"]
    C -.-> G["Receiver type stays exactly as its author wrote it"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="What a holder contributes" id="holder">
        <p>
            A holder is a class marked <code>@Extension</code>. Every <code>public</code> method
            of it is a candidate. Anything less visible stays an ordinary helper. Where
            <code>@Receiver</code> sits decides the kind.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>@Receiver sits on</td>
                <td>You get</td>
                <td>Called as</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>the first parameter</td>
                <td>an instance method</td>
                <td><code>value.name(...)</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>the method</td>
                <td>a static method</td>
                <td><code>Type.name(...)</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>a public static final field</td>
                <td>a constant</td>
                <td><code>Type.NAME</code></td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            <code>@Extension(BigDecimal.class)</code> names one receiver type for the whole class. A
            method naming none of its own takes it as parameter zero. A holder that is not
            <code>final</code> is <code>AW1300</code>, a warning. Its members are contributed
            anyway. Declare it final; nothing ever instantiates it.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                A weave adds a member from the inside, to a target it rewrites. An extension
                changes no target at all. <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> has the
                four shapes that takes.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="One manifest, two lists" id="manifest">
        <p>
            The processor records each contribution in the generated weave manifest, which
            <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> covers. There,
            <code>weaves</code> and <code>extensions</code> are separate arrays. Three things
            follow from that.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>A module can declare extensions and no weave at all. The build-time driver
                still runs. It repoints your calls and, where this module also declares a
                holder, hardens that holder's own null checks.</p></li>
            <li><p>Extensions are read from every entry of the compile classpath. Weaves are read
                from this module's own output. So a dependency's extension reaches your code where
                its weave would not. <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> puts that
                beside the other drivers.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>require</code> and <code>scope</code> are read off the holder. Both
                apply to every entry it writes. Neither has a per-member override.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What the rewrite touches" id="rewrite">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="The caller's instructions">
                <p>
                    An <code>invokevirtual</code> or <code>invokeinterface</code> on an instance
                    extension becomes an <code>invokestatic</code> on the holder. The receiver is
                    passed as argument zero. An <code>invokestatic</code> on a static extension is
                    repointed the same way. Nothing is prepended: its descriptor already has no
                    receiver parameter. A <code>getstatic</code> of a contributed constant is
                    repointed at the holder's field. A method handle among an
                    <code>invokedynamic</code>'s bootstrap arguments is rebound the same way. That
                    is what carries a method reference.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Never the receiver type">
                <p>
                    The type that appears to gain the member is not rewritten. Only the patched
                    copy the compiler saw declares it. That copy is a build artefact.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A real member, first">
                <p>
                    Resolution starts at the owner written at the call site: superclasses first,
                    then interfaces. A static extension is the exception: it stops at the
                    superclass chain. One declared on an interface is never reached from a call on
                    an implementing class. The first type to answer ends the walk. A member the
                    hierarchy genuinely declares beats an extension. That is guaranteed by
                    <code>AW1309</code>, and only where the receiver type was on the classpath when
                    the index was built. Where a supertype cannot be read, the call is left alone.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A receiver you asked to be checked">
                <p>
                    <code>@Receiver(nulls = Nulls.CHECKED)</code> puts a
                    <code>requireNonNull</code> prologue into the holder's own method. An
                    <code>invokestatic</code> on a null receiver throws nothing by itself. Only an
                    instance contribution may ask for it. On a static one or on a constant it is
                    <code>AW1315</code>. Remove <code>nulls</code>, or mark a parameter
                    <code>@Receiver</code> instead.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Only the build rewrites anything" id="build">
        <p>
            Only the Maven plugin gives the engine an index of extensions. The agent, the
            weaving class loader and the testkit all leave it empty. No call site they see is
            repointed. Where the pass sits among the gates is in
            <a href="pipeline.topic">the weaving pipeline</a>.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="require">
                <p>
                    <code>REQUIRED</code> is the default. The receiver type must be on the compile
                    classpath or in the runtime image. Absent from both, stub generation fails. Add
                    the dependency that provides it, or declare
                    <code>require = Require.OPTIONAL</code> to skip the contribution.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="scope">
                <p>
                    <code>MODULE</code> withholds the stub from everyone but the declaring
                    module. It is read where stubs are written, and nowhere else. Nothing at weave
                    time consults it. A call site that did compile is rewritten anyway.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <warning>
            <p>
                A method stub throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>. A constant stub
                is a plain field carrying the holder's value, so a contributed constant read
                succeeds silently instead. Keep the stub tree off any runtime classpath either
                way. A stub there shadows the real receiver type.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What is refused" id="refused">
        <p>
            The holder is checked before its members. Two of these stop the pass outright.
            <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostics</a> covers what the ranges mean.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="A holder with type parameters, or with a supertype">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1306</code> and <code>AW1307</code>, and each ends the pass. Remove the
                    type parameters; make the class extend <code>Object</code> and implement
                    nothing. Nothing about the holder participates at the call site.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A public method that is not static">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1301</code>. Declare it static, or make it private if it is a
                    helper. The receiver arrives as a parameter. A contributed method has no
                    instance of its own.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A member the receiver type already declares">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1305</code> from the processor, for any kind of extension. A method
                    collision is caught again at index build, as <code>AW1309</code>. A constant
                    colliding with a field is not — the index checks methods only. There,
                    <code>find()</code> resolves the call to the field that was already there.
                    Rename the extension. <a href="redirect-and-wrap.topic">Redirect and Wrap</a>
                    is the mechanism for changing what an existing member does.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Two contributions of one call">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1308</code>, from two overloads that erase to one descriptor. Two
                    artefacts contributing the same call get it too. Rename one of them. Otherwise
                    the winner depends on classpath order.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A receiver type that cannot carry a member">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1304</code> for a primitive, an array or a type variable.
                    <code>AW1311</code> for a parameterised type, erasure being all the call site
                    has. Name a class or interface. Use the raw type where it is generic.
                    <code>Object</code> is <code>AW1312</code>, a warning: name the narrowest type
                    the member is meaningful on.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

